<title>SOUTH KOREA: South Korea's oil desulphurising plans-table.</title>
<headline>South Korea's oil desulphurising plans-table.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-04-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>South Korean refiners face a dilemma over how to match up to tougher pollution laws that will be implemented in coming years, industry sources said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>The new laws will demand lower levels of harmful sulphur in oil products for the domestic market.</p>
<p>The refiners, who process 2.44 million barrels per day (bpd) of crude, could invest in new refinery units to take out the sulphur, or buy more crude oil that is low in sulphur to start with.</p>
<p>But both options will be expensive, analysts said.  </p>
<p>The following table shows South Korea's current desulphurising capacity and known plans for the future:</p>
<p>(Ministry of Trade, Industry and Energy, in barrels per day)</p>
<p>MIDDLE DISTILLATE DESULPHURISERS:</p>
<p>				 current capacity   planned</p>
<p>Yukong			     177,000</p>
<p>LG-Caltex			  120,000	  60,000 (early '98)</p>
<p>Ssangyong			  115,000</p>
<p>Hanwha				38,000	  50,000 (late '97)</p>
<p>Hyundai			     99,000</p>
<p>TOTAL				549,000	 110,000</p>
<p>HEAVY FUEL OIL DESULPHURISERS:</p>
<p>				 current capacity   planned</p>
<p>Yukong				40,000</p>
<p>LG-Caltex			   none	    60,000 (early 2000)</p>
<p>Ssangyong			   25,000</p>
<p>Hanwha				none	    30,000 (late '99)</p>
<p>Hyundai			     none</p>
<p>TOTAL				 65,000	  90,000</p>
<p>--Seoul Newsroom (+822 727 5643)</p>
